(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["phy-11-07"] = {"phy-11-07-m01":{"idea":"A wave carries energy from place to place. The material of the medium stays where it is.","steps":["Each particle of the medium only moves a little about its mean position.","It passes the disturbance to the next particle.","So energy travels along with the wave, but matter does not."],"others":"If matter also moved, a wave would carry the water or air away, which does not happen. Matter without energy, or neither, cannot describe a wave.","trap":"A cork on a water wave bobs up and down; it does not travel with the wave.","answer":"energy, but not the matter of the medium"},"phy-11-07-m02":{"idea":"A transverse wave has particle motion at a right angle to the direction of travel.","steps":["Think of a wave on a string: the string moves up and down.","The wave itself moves along the string.","So the vibration is at right angles to the direction of the wave."],"others":"Vibration along the direction is a longitudinal wave. Gases cannot carry a transverse wave. Circles around the source do not describe the motion of particles in a wave.","trap":"Transverse: right angles. Longitudinal: along the line.","answer":"at right angles to the direction in which the wave moves"},"phy-11-07-m03":{"idea":"Sound in air is a longitudinal wave made of squeezed and stretched regions of air.","steps":["Air particles move back and forth along the direction in which the sound travels.","Where they bunch up, the pressure is high: a compression.","Where they spread out, the pressure is low: a rarefaction.","So sound is longitudinal, with compressions and rarefactions."],"others":"Crests and troughs belong to transverse waves. Calling sound transverse is wrong, because air cannot carry a shear.","trap":"Compressions and rarefactions go with longitudinal waves.","answer":"longitudinal, made of compressions and rarefactions"},"phy-11-07-m04":{"idea":"Wave speed equals frequency times wavelength.","steps":["In one period T the wave moves forward by one wavelength λ.","So v = λ/T.","Since T = 1/n, v = n λ."],"others":"n/λ and n + λ have wrong units. λ/n² is also not a speed.","trap":"When a wave enters a new medium, frequency stays the same and wavelength changes.","answer":"v = nλ"},"phy-11-07-m05":{"idea":"The sign between kx and ωt tells which way a wave moves.","steps":["Follow a point of constant phase: kx − ωt = constant.","As t grows, x must grow too to keep the phase fixed.","So the wave moves in the positive x direction, with speed ω/k."],"others":"A wave along the y axis would not depend on x. A standing wave has the form sin kx cos ωt. Travelling in the negative direction needs a plus sign: kx + ωt.","trap":"Minus sign: moves to +x. Plus sign: moves to −x.","answer":"travelling in the positive x direction"},"phy-11-07-m06":{"idea":"Compare the given wave with y = a sin(kx − ωt) to read off k and ω.","steps":["From y = 0.02 sin(3x − 900t): k = 3 rad/m and ω = 900 rad/s.","The speed is v = ω/k.","v = 900/3 = 300 m/s."],"others":"2700 comes from multiplying 3 and 900. 270 and 0.0033 come from other wrong combinations of the numbers.","trap":"Speed is ω divided by k, not multiplied.","answer":"300 m/s"},"phy-11-07-m07":{"idea":"Wave speed on a string rises with tension and falls with mass per unit length.","steps":["A larger tension pulls a displaced piece back harder, so the wave is faster.","A heavier string has more inertia, so the wave is slower.","Putting both together: v = √(T/m)."],"others":"√(Tm) makes the wave faster on a heavier string. T/m has the wrong dimensions. √(m/T) turns the formula upside down.","trap":"Tension on top, mass per length below, under a square root.","answer":"√(T/m)"},"phy-11-07-m08":{"idea":"Speed on a string goes as the square root of the tension.","steps":["Speed on a string goes as v ∝ √T.","The tension becomes 4T.","So v becomes √4 times the old speed, which is 2v."],"others":"Four times would mean v ∝ T. Half and unchanged do not fit a stronger pull.","trap":"A square root halves an effect: ×4 in tension gives only ×2 in speed.","answer":"twice as large"},"phy-11-07-m09":{"idea":"Laplace corrected Newton: sound compresses air so fast that no heat flows, so γ appears.","steps":["Newton assumed the temperature stays constant and got v = √(P/ρ).","That gives about 280 m/s, which is too low.","Laplace said the compressions are adiabatic.","This adds the factor γ: v = √(γP/ρ)."],"others":"√(P/ρ) is Newton's value. √(ρ/γP) and γP/ρ have the wrong form for a speed.","trap":"γ is about 1.4 for air.","answer":"v = √(γP/ρ)"},"phy-11-07-m10":{"idea":"At constant temperature, raising the pressure of a gas raises its density in the same ratio.","steps":["Sound speed is v = √(γP/ρ).","By Boyle's law, P ∝ ρ at constant temperature, so P/ρ stays constant.","So v stays the same."],"others":"Pressure alone does not raise the speed, because the density rises too. A larger density does not lower the speed, since P rises equally. Doubling P does not double v.","trap":"Sound speed in a gas depends on temperature, not on pressure.","answer":"unchanged, as the density changes in the same ratio as the pressure"},"phy-11-07-m11":{"idea":"Water vapour is lighter than air, so moist air is a little less dense.","steps":["Water molecules have mass 18; nitrogen is 28 and oxygen is 32.","Replacing some air with vapour lowers the density at the same pressure.","In v = √(γP/ρ), a smaller ρ gives a larger v."],"others":"Moist air does not have lower pressure or more particles per unit volume at the same conditions. It is also not denser, because water vapour is lighter.","trap":"Sound travels slightly faster in humid air.","answer":"moist air is less dense, as water vapour is lighter than air"},"phy-11-07-m12":{"idea":"Sound speed in air rises by a small fixed amount for each degree.","steps":["The formula is v = 331 + 0.61 t, with t in °C.","At 0 °C it is 331 m/s; at 20 °C it is about 343 m/s.","So the rise is 0.61 m/s for each °C."],"others":"61, 6.1 and 0.061 m/s per degree are off by a factor of 100, 10 and 10.","trap":"Remember 0.61 m/s per °C.","answer":"0.61 m/s for each degree Celsius"},"phy-11-07-m13":{"idea":"When waves overlap, the displacements add.","steps":["Two waves meet at a point.","Each would give its own displacement there.","The resultant displacement is the sum of the two, with signs.","After passing, each wave goes on unchanged."],"others":"They do not always cancel. The resultant is not the larger of the two or their product.","trap":"Add with signs: two displacements can partly or fully cancel.","answer":"the sum of the displacements that each wave would produce alone"},"phy-11-07-m14":{"idea":"Two waves in phase give an amplitude equal to the sum of their amplitudes.","steps":["Phase difference is zero, so crest meets crest.","The displacements add: 3 + 4 = 7 cm."],"others":"5 cm is for a phase difference of 90°. 12 cm is the product. 1 cm is for opposite phase.","trap":"In phase: add. Opposite phase: subtract.","answer":"7 cm"},"phy-11-07-m15":{"idea":"The ear keeps a sound for about 0.1 s, so an echo must come back after at least that time.","steps":["Sound must travel to the wall and back.","In 0.1 s at 340 m/s it covers 34 m.","That is the two-way distance, so the wall must be at least 17 m away."],"others":"34 m would be right for the full round trip, not the distance to the wall. 340 m and 1.7 m use the wrong time.","trap":"Echo distance is half of speed × time.","answer":"17 m away, as the ear keeps a sound for about 0.1 s"},"phy-11-07-m16":{"idea":"Reverberation is sound that lingers in a hall after the source stops.","steps":["Sound reflects many times from walls, ceiling and floor.","These reflections arrive one after another, so the sound lingers.","That lingering is reverberation."],"others":"A change in the frequency heard is the Doppler effect. Speed of sound in a hot room is a different matter. Two waves cancelling is destructive interference.","trap":"Curtains and carpets absorb sound and reduce reverberation.","answer":"the persistence of sound after the source has stopped, because of many reflections"},"phy-11-07-m17":{"idea":"Pitch is how shrill or deep a note is, and it depends on frequency.","steps":["A higher frequency gives a shriller, higher-pitched note.","Loudness depends on amplitude, and quality depends on overtones.","So pitch depends on frequency."],"others":"Amplitude sets loudness. Overtones set quality, which is why a flute and a violin sound different. Wave speed does not set the pitch.","trap":"Pitch = frequency, loudness = amplitude, timbre = overtones.","answer":"frequency, and a higher frequency gives a shriller note"},"phy-11-07-m18":{"idea":"Sound level in decibels grows with the logarithm of intensity.","steps":["Level L = 10 log₁₀(I/I₀).","A factor of 100 in intensity gives 10 log₁₀ 100 = 10 × 2.","So the level rises by 20 dB."],"others":"2 dB uses the log without the factor 10. 100 dB confuses the factor with dB. 10 dB is for a factor of 10 only.","trap":"Every factor of 10 in intensity adds 10 dB.","answer":"20 dB"},"phy-11-07-m19":{"idea":"The Doppler effect is a change in heard frequency because of relative motion.","steps":["If the source and the listener come closer, the heard frequency is higher.","If they move apart, it is lower.","So the cause is relative motion between source and listener."],"others":"Changing the amplitude changes loudness, not pitch. Two at rest give no shift. Heating the medium changes the speed of sound, not a Doppler shift.","trap":"The speed of sound in the medium does not change with the motion of the source.","answer":"the source and the listener are in relative motion"},"phy-11-07-m20":{"idea":"A moving source crowds the waves in front of it, so the heard frequency rises.","steps":["The source follows its own waves.","In front of it the crests come closer: the wavelength is shorter.","Sound speed in air is unchanged, so n = v/λ becomes higher."],"others":"A longer wavelength in front would give a lower frequency. Equal frequency is wrong for a moving source. The speed of sound in air does not rise.","trap":"Source approaching: shorter wavelength in front, higher pitch.","answer":"higher, and the wavelength in the air in front of the source is shorter"},"phy-11-07-m21":{"idea":"A receding source stretches the waves, so the frequency heard is lower.","steps":["The source moves away at v_s, so the wavelength becomes (v + v_s)/n₀.","The frequency heard is the speed of sound divided by this wavelength.","n = n₀ v/(v + v_s)."],"others":"n₀ v/(v − v_s) is for an approaching source. The other two multiply by a factor that does not come from the wavelength.","trap":"Receding: plus in the denominator. Approaching: minus.","answer":"n₀ v/(v + v_s)"},"phy-11-07-m22":{"idea":"A listener moving towards the source meets the crests faster.","steps":["The wavelength λ = v/n₀ is unchanged, because the source is still.","The listener meets crests at the rate (v + v_L)/λ.","So n = n₀ (v + v_L)/v, which is higher than n₀."],"others":"(v − v_L)/v is for a listener moving away. The forms with v in the numerator and a v_L term in the denominator belong to a moving source.","trap":"Listener moves: change the numerator. Source moves: change the denominator.","answer":"n₀ (v + v_L)/v"},"phy-11-07-m23":{"idea":"A steady interference pattern needs coherent sources.","steps":["Coherent means the same frequency and a fixed phase difference.","Then the loud and soft places stay in the same positions.","Different frequencies or a changing phase make the pattern move or fade."],"others":"Different frequencies shift the pattern with time. A quickly changing phase also destroys steadiness. Equal amplitude with different speed is not the condition.","trap":"Remember: same frequency and fixed phase difference.","answer":"the same frequency, and a fixed phase difference"},"phy-11-07-n01":{"idea":"Read k and ω from the wave equation, then use v = ω/k.","steps":["Compare y = 0.02 sin(3x − 900t) with y = a sin(kx − ωt).","So k = 3 rad/m and ω = 900 rad/s.","v = ω/k = 900/3 = 300 m/s."],"trap":"Check: in 1 ms a crest moves 0.3 m, which is 300 m/s.","answer":"300 m/s"},"phy-11-07-n02":{"idea":"Use v = √(T/m) for waves on a string.","steps":["T = 100 N and m = 0.040 kg/m.","T/m = 100/0.040 = 2500.","v = √2500 = 50.0 m/s."],"trap":"Check units: N/(kg/m) = m²/s², so the root is m/s.","answer":"50.0 m/s"},"phy-11-07-n03":{"idea":"Use the rule v = 331 + 0.61 t for sound in air.","steps":["t = 25 °C.","v = 331 + 0.61 × 25 = 331 + 15.25.","v = 346.25 m/s, about 346 m/s."],"trap":"Check with v ∝ √T: 331 × √(298/273) is about 346 m/s.","answer":"346 m/s"},"phy-11-07-n04":{"idea":"Use Laplace's formula v = √(γP/ρ).","steps":["γ = 1.4, P = 1.013 × 10⁵ Pa, ρ = 1.29 kg/m³.","γP/ρ = 1.4 × 1.013 × 10⁵/1.29 = 1.099 × 10⁵.","v = √(1.099 × 10⁵) = 332 m/s."],"trap":"Check: this is close to the usual 331 m/s at 0 °C.","answer":"332 m/s"},"phy-11-07-n05":{"idea":"An echo travels to the cliff and back, so the distance doubles.","steps":["Total distance = 2 × 170 = 340 m.","Time = distance/speed = 340/340.","t = 1.00 s."],"trap":"Do not use 170 m alone: the sound goes there and back.","answer":"1.00 s"},"phy-11-07-n06":{"idea":"A point source spreads sound over a sphere, so intensity falls as 1/r².","steps":["Triple the distance: intensity becomes 1/9.","Fall in level = 10 log₁₀ 9.","log₁₀ 9 = 0.954, so the fall is 9.54 dB."],"trap":"Same as 20 log₁₀ 3 = 9.54 dB.","answer":"9.54 dB"},"phy-11-07-n07":{"idea":"For a source moving towards a still listener, n = n₀ v/(v − v_s).","steps":["n₀ = 500 Hz, v = 340 m/s, v_s = 20 m/s.","n = 500 × 340/(340 − 20) = 500 × 340/320.","n = 531.25 Hz."],"trap":"The heard frequency must be higher than 500 Hz because the source approaches.","answer":"531.25 Hz"},"phy-11-07-n08":{"idea":"When both move towards each other, the numerator grows and the denominator shrinks.","steps":["n = n₀ (v + v_L)/(v − v_s).","v + v_L = 340 + 10 = 350 and v − v_s = 340 − 20 = 320.","n = 500 × 350/320 = 546.88 Hz."],"trap":"Approaching sets: listener adds to v, source subtracts from v.","answer":"546.88 Hz"},"phy-11-07-n09":{"idea":"For two waves with a phase difference φ, the resultant amplitude comes from the cosine rule.","steps":["A = √(a² + b² + 2ab cos φ).","a = 3, b = 4, φ = 60°, cos 60° = 0.5.","A = √(9 + 16 + 24 × 0.5) = √37.","A = 6.08 cm."],"trap":"The answer lies between 1 cm (opposite phase) and 7 cm (in phase).","answer":"6.08 cm"},"phy-11-07-n10":{"idea":"The wave number k and the wavelength are linked by k = 2π/λ.","steps":["Here k = 4.0 rad/m.","λ = 2π/k = 2 × 3.1416/4.0.","λ = 1.571 m."],"trap":"k is in radians per metre, so the 2π is needed.","answer":"1.571 m"},"phy-11-07-n11":{"idea":"Sonar echo: the pulse goes down and comes back, so the depth is half of speed × time.","steps":["Round trip distance = v t = 1500 × 0.80 = 1200 m.","Depth d = 1200/2 = 600 m."],"trap":"Do not forget to halve the distance.","answer":"600 m"}};
